import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Runtime","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/explanation/runtime.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/explanation/runtime.md"}`),a={name:`handl/explanation/runtime.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="runtime" tabindex="-1">Runtime <a class="header-anchor" href="#runtime" aria-label="Permalink to “Runtime”">​</a></h1><p>The handl runtime is not a single, hard-coded VM. It is the set of operation plugins that a <code>Host</code> loads and executes, plus the engine that evaluates <code>Term</code>s. There is no separate &quot;VM&quot; subsystem — evaluation is the <code>Eval</code> operation, served like any other operation.</p><h2 id="the-execution-model" tabindex="-1">The execution model <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-execution-model" aria-label="Permalink to “The execution model”">​</a></h2><p>A <code>Host</code> loads kits, resolves dependencies, and runs <code>contribute</code> to a fixed point. After loading, <code>Host::execute</code> dispatches a <code>CapabilityRequest</code> to the plugin that provides the requested capability.</p><p>The language pipeline is also a set of operation executions. A host that loads the lex, parse, typecheck, and eval contract surfaces can run those operations by executing the corresponding operation plugin — each one an engine parameterized by the same <code>Language</code> data (see <a href="./language-as-data.html">Language as data</a>).</p><h2 id="terms-and-values" tabindex="-1">Terms and values <a class="header-anchor" href="#terms-and-values" aria-label="Permalink to “Terms and values”">​</a></h2><p>Programs reach the engines as <code>Term</code>s — nodes in the closed arrow calculus — over the shared <code>Value</code> representation (both defined in the <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/kernel" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>kernel</code></a> repo). The engine layer evaluates <code>Term</code>s against the <code>Language</code>&#39;s <code>eval</code> schemes; the reference interpreter is <code>hnl-engine-exec-tree-walk</code>, which walks the <code>Term</code> graph directly.</p><h2 id="operations-and-engines" tabindex="-1">Operations and engines <a class="header-anchor" href="#operations-and-engines" aria-label="Permalink to “Operations and engines”">​</a></h2><p>Evaluation is the <code>Eval</code> operation. <code>handl-platform</code> defines the <code>Eval</code> contract (<code>Eval</code>/<code>EvalRequest</code>/<code>EvalBehavior</code>) and <code>BasicEvaluator</code>, the <code>RuleAcceptor</code> that collects one behavior per <code>AstNode.kind</code>; the <code>handl-plugin-ext-eval</code> crate is the published capability surface. A compiler target does not use <code>Eval</code> — it uses the <code>Compile</code> operation with <code>CompileBehavior</code> rules instead, since compilation processes the whole program rather than evaluating per node. &quot;Targets are plugins&quot; holds in both cases: an interpreter target is a set of <code>Eval</code> rules; a compiler target is a set of <code>Compile</code> rules.</p><p>Modules — capability namespaces such as filesystem, env, or clock access — are contributed by library plugins through the <code>Module</code>/<code>Function</code> traits in <code>handl-platform</code>. The host does not hard-code a module list; it discovers modules through the loaded plugins.</p><h2 id="garbage-collection" tabindex="-1">Garbage collection <a class="header-anchor" href="#garbage-collection" aria-label="Permalink to “Garbage collection”">​</a></h2><p>Garbage collection is provided by a target or library plugin and selected by the host. The kernel imposes no GC. A host may run with no GC, a reference-counting GC, a tracing GC, or a region-based collector — all are plugin choices. See design principle #15.</p><h2 id="sandboxing" tabindex="-1">Sandboxing <a class="header-anchor" href="#sandboxing" aria-label="Permalink to “Sandboxing”">​</a></h2><p>Whether a program runs sandboxed is determined by which modules are exposed and which engine runs it, not by a kernel flag. A kit that exposes only pure modules and selects a non-IO engine produces a sandbox. The kernel has no sandbox concept. See design principle #16.</p><h2 id="capabilities" tabindex="-1">Capabilities <a class="header-anchor" href="#capabilities" aria-label="Permalink to “Capabilities”">​</a></h2><p>The same program can run with:</p><ul><li>real OS services in production,</li><li>in-memory stubs in tests,</li><li>browser or embedded implementations in other contexts.</li></ul><p>Which one a given host uses is determined by the target and library plugins it loads.</p><h2 id="errors" tabindex="-1">Errors <a class="header-anchor" href="#errors" aria-label="Permalink to “Errors”">​</a></h2><p>Errors are reported as structured values with a kind and message. The <code>plugin-ext-diag</code> contract surface defines the shared <code>Diagnostic</code>, <code>Severity</code>, <code>Span</code>, and <code>Fix</code> vocabulary every operation renders through.</p>`,20)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};